    "markdown": "# Knowledge Base: cantor-diagonalization-oq-03-oq-01-oq-02\n\nInsights accumulated during research on this problem.\n\n---\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\nThis problem asks for Rice's theorem as an instance of Lawvere's fixed-point theorem. The target statement is that every non-trivial semantic property of computable partial functions is undecidable: for a property P depending only on program behavior, the set of codes e with P(phi_e) cannot be decided by a total computable procedure.\n\n---\n\n## Local Evidence\n\n- problem.md names the target as \"Rice's Theorem as Lawvere Instance\" and lists the parent Lawvere fixed-point theorem proof cantor-diagonalization-oq-03-oq-01 as already proven.\n- state.md keeps the work in OBSERVE on the fast path with zero total attempts.\n- Proofs/CantorDiagonalizationOQ03OQ01OQ02.lean contains an abstract Rice/Lawvere development, including abstract_rice, rice_induced_obstruction, semantic_rice_flip, no_halting_model, and classical_rice_abstract.\n\n---\n\n## Open Work\n\nThe concrete Lean derivation for computable partial functions remains open in the problem notes. The main missing bridge is a usable computability model: program codes, semantics, semantic equivalence, non-trivial semantic properties, and the Lawvere point-surjectivity or Kleene recursion-theorem infrastructure needed to derive the classical Rice conclusion.\n"
  },
